# Loops in C++ ⚡

C++ gives you tight control over memory and performance, so its loops are versatile and fast. Here's the full toolkit:

## 1. **Classic `for` Loop**
The traditional loop with init, condition, and update.
```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    std::cout << i << " "; // 0 1 2 3 4
}
```

## 2. **`while` Loop**
Runs while a condition is true.
```cpp
int count = 0;
while (count < 5) {
    std::cout << count << " ";
    count++;
}
```

## 3. **`do-while` Loop**
Executes **at least once**, then checks the condition.
```cpp
int num = 0;
do {
    std::cout << num << " ";
    num++;
} while (num < 5);
```

## 4. **Range-Based `for` Loop** *(C++11)*
Clean way to iterate over arrays, vectors, strings, etc.
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

std::vector<int> nums = {10, 20, 30, 40};

for (int n : nums) {
    std::cout << n << " "; // 10 20 30 40
}

// Modify elements by reference
for (int &n : nums) {
    n *= 2;
}
```

## 5. **Iterators with `for`**
Common when working with STL containers.
```cpp
#include <list>

std::list<std::string> names = {"Alice", "Bob", "Charlie"};

for (auto it = names.begin(); it != names.end(); ++it) {
    std::cout << *it << " ";
}
```

---

## Control Keywords
| Keyword | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| **`break`** | Exit the loop immediately |
| **`continue`** | Skip to the next iteration |

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    if (i % 2 == 0) continue;  // Skip even numbers
    if (i > 7) break;          // Stop at 8
    std::cout << i << " ";     // Prints: 1 3 5 7
}
```

---

## Performance Tips ⚙️
C++ lets you optimize loops for speed:

### ✅ **Use `const auto&` for large objects**
```cpp
std::vector<std::string> words = /* ... */;

// Bad: Copies each string
for (std::string w : words) { ... }

// Good: Avoids copy, read-only
for (const auto& w : words) { ... }

// Best: Allows modification without copying
for (auto& w : words) { ... }
```

### ✅ **Prefer range-based `for` over iterators**
Cleaner and just as fast.

### ✅ **Avoid unnecessary work in loop conditions**
```cpp
// ❌ Inefficient: re-evaluates size() every iteration
for (int i = 0; i < vec.size(); i++) { ... }

// ✅ Better: compute once
size_t n = vec.size();
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { ... }
```

---

## Nested Loops Example
```cpp
// Print a 5x5 grid
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < 5; j++) {
        std::cout << "(" << i << "," << j << ") ";
    }
    std::cout << "\n";
}
```

---

## Quick Comparison
| Loop Type | Best For |
|-----------|----------|
| `for` | Fixed iterations, counting |
| `while` | Unknown iterations |
| `do-while` | Must run once first |
| Range-based `for` | Collections, arrays, strings |
| Iterator `for` | STL containers (legacy/codebases) |

---

C++ loops are powerful because they combine low-level control with modern convenience. What specific use case are you working on? 😊
